<div class="prose series-introduction"><p>The Northstar incident now has an evidence-bounded assessment: output {{CASE_OUTPUT}} omitted {{CASE_MISSING_COUNT}} required identifiers under the accepted completeness definition. Cause and recovery remain unestablished. The agent has also prepared candidate code revision {{CASE_FIX_REVISION}} as a possible response that needs evaluation.</p><p><strong>Lifecycle</strong> lets the owner read each subject’s state and permitted next step. The agent recovers how the completeness definition was accepted before the incident, then checks the candidate change, open response and usability of its task information separately.</p></div>
<section class="article-section" id="incident-case" aria-labelledby="incident-case-heading"><h2 id="incident-case-heading">The same incident at a review checkpoint</h2>{{INCIDENT_CASE}}</section>
<section class="article-section" id="lifecycle-definition" aria-labelledby="lifecycle-definition-heading"><h2 id="lifecycle-definition-heading">What lifecycle means</h2>
{{LIFECYCLE_DEFINITION}}
</section>
{{VISUAL_LIFECYCLE}}
<section class="article-section" id="change" aria-labelledby="change-title"><h2 id="change-title">Make the effects of a change explicit</h2>
<h3 class="article-subheading">Review the requested effect</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>A reviewer examines candidate revision {{CASE_FIX_REVISION}}, its exact contents, evidence, requested effects and authority for accepting them. The initial investigation permission covers preparing a proposal. Adoption and activation follow their own authorization routes. A <strong>Decision Bundle</strong> lets distinct decisions share a review; each retains its owner, targets and limits.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading">Apply authorized record changes</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>After authorization, an <strong>Admission Transaction</strong> applies the accepted records and their declared consequences together. It records the resulting <strong>Project State</strong>: the project’s saved records and knowledge. Inclusion in that state preserves each record’s standing; it does not automatically accept a proposal, activate software or close an incident.</p></div>
{{FIGURE_ADMISSION}}
<h3 class="article-subheading">Preserve both histories</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>One history preserves what changed. A separately linked history preserves why those changes were accepted. Deployment, recorded activation and observed operation have their own requirements. The local admission mechanism applies declared, authorized effects within its scope.</p></div>
{{SOURCES_LIFECYCLE}}
</section>
<section class="article-section" id="kinds-of-change" aria-labelledby="kinds-of-change-title"><h2 id="kinds-of-change-title">Three kinds of change</h2>
<div class="prose"><p>The Lab distinguishes a changed meaning, a changed encoding and a rebuilt view. These distinctions keep a format update from silently changing the rule it represents. A <strong>canonical record</strong> is the designated primary representation for its scope; a derived view is rebuilt from that record.</p></div>
<div class="series-change-cards"><div><span class="record-kind">Semantic Epoch</span><h3>Changed meaning</h3><p>An owner-determined material change to meaning, scope or semantic requirements needs a successor meaning version and explicit owner selection.</p></div><div><span class="record-kind">Representation Revision</span><h3>Changed encoding</h3><p>A new machine encoding preserves meaning with equivalence evidence, validation and owner selection. The earlier revision remains identifiable.</p></div><div><span class="record-kind">Derived Refresh</span><h3>Rebuilt view</h3><p>Regenerate and validate a view from unchanged canonical inputs under an exact generator binding. This creates no new meaning or authority; a generator change is governed separately.</p></div></div>
<details class="facet-details"><summary>How someone can challenge an accepted record</summary><p class="small">A <strong>Challenge Contract</strong> states who may contest a revision, acceptable reasons and evidence, and who must respond. A <strong>Challenge Event</strong> records a particular challenge. Filing it does not automatically suspend or withdraw the record. Those changes follow the contract and require an authorized decision.</p></details>
</section>
<section class="article-section" id="lifecycle-agent" aria-labelledby="lifecycle-agent-title"><h2 id="lifecycle-agent-title">What the next agent receives</h2>
<h3 class="article-subheading">Recover durable project knowledge</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>A machine-agent instance belongs to one session and ends with it. Its working memory is temporary. Durable continuity comes from saved Project State, through a <strong>Context View</strong> prepared for the task and an <strong>Ontology View</strong> of relevant concepts and connections.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading">Refresh task information before continuing</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>Task view {{CASE_VIEW_VERSION}} was usable when this investigation began. At a later checkpoint its period of use has ended, so the agent pauses until a newly checked view and current permission are available. A new session starts with empty working memory and recovers the saved assessment, candidate proposal and owner decisions through current views.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading">Keep the response accountable</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>The incident record’s lifecycle is active, while its response closure field remains open. A closure decision follows the incident’s governing Policy and preserves uncertainty and follow-up obligations. Recovery may be evidenced while the cause remains unresolved.</p></div>
{{INCIDENT_LENS_SUMMARY}}
<p class="series-bridge">Read the three questions together: authority identifies what governs the work, provenance makes its origins inspectable and lifecycle identifies the state and route for change.</p>
<a class="lens-continue" href="governance-lab-reference.html#purpose">Continue to the complete Lab model →</a>
{{SOURCES_LIFECYCLE_AGENT}}
</section>
